## The definitive acceptance test

I would give the coding agent exactly this test.

Start the MCP server from a clean install and ask:

```text
Resolve:
hear
speak
glow
pan
tilt
wifi

quantity = 1
budget = $50
optimize = lowest_integration
```

A passing result must:

```text
✓ satisfy every requested capability
✓ not count a multifunction module multiple times
✓ contain two independent motion axes if needed
✓ contain a compute/control path
✓ contain no bare IC unless route says custom-PCBA
✓ stay under $50 total
✓ use quantity-1 prices
✓ return nonempty supplier SKU for every part
✓ return evidence/source for every part
✓ contain no hard compatibility failures
✓ persist the resolve observation
✓ save/reload build across process restart
✓ reprice it
✓ be callable through actual MCP
```

Then run:

```text
same request
quantity = 100
```

and verify the architecture **can differ** rather than simply multiplying the quantity-one retail route by 100.

That second test is where this starts becoming the product we actually want.

### Bottom line

The coding agent got the **shape** right extremely quickly: adapters → canonical-ish data → resolver → MCP-ish layer → tests. Keep that.

But right now the repo's strongest claims are ahead of the implementation:

```text
"3 adapters"
→ really fixture adapters

"MCP server"
→ really Python service class

"compatibility"
→ mostly non-enforcing

"resolver"
→ independent capability picker

"evidence"
→ generic labels

"data garden"
→ not persisted
```

So **do not expand scope**. This next push should make this tiny kernel brutally real.

Once the six-capability desk-head request works correctly against even **one live supplier plus our verified module fixtures**, then adding LCSC/Waveshare/M5Stack/Seeed/AliExpress becomes straightforward adapter work rather than multiplying errors.
